/*  skipT -- THE LINE-COMMENT RULE CAN BE WRITTEN, AND WHAT IT CONSUMES CANNOT YET BE READ.

    THE BLOCKER, measured 2026-09-17 (docs/checkSKIP.md section 2a): the two-character literal
    that spells a line comment KILLS THE DEFINE IT IS WRITTEN IN. There is no lexer, so the
    parser reads it as a comment inside its own source and eats the rest of the line, closing
    quote and semicolon included. The rest of the checkSKIP rule set is innocent -- the block
    delimiters are fine and were never the problem.

    THE RESPELL, and it is an ESCAPE rather than two literals. Three spellings parse; SK-1 and
    SK-2 are why one of them is preferred.

    ⚠ SK-1/SK-2 ARE THE WHOLE ARGUMENT FOR THE ESCAPE FORM AND THEY ARE A MEASUREMENT, NOT A
    TASTE. The escaped spelling yields ONE literal term matching both characters. The two-literal
    spelling yields TWO terms of one character each -- and terms are skip points, so a skipper
    is entitled to run between them. That is a DIFFERENT RULE wearing the same intent, and it is
    the one that would quietly accept a slash, a space and a slash.

    ⚠ AND WHAT THIS FIXTURE CANNOT ASSERT, STATED RATHER THAN OMITTED: WHAT THE RULE CONSUMES.
    A rule driven standalone has no enclosing activation to take the label it mints -- the run
    says so, `checkInput: no enclosing activation to take the label` -- so after the drive its
    terms still read their own DEFINITIONS and not matched data. That is fixIts F-83 arriving
    through a second door, and it is STATION 6's. SK-5 pins it so the day it changes, something
    says so.
    ⚠ Nor is anything ROUTING a skip through these rules: the live skipper is the C++
    `checkSkip`, and `sKIP`/`checkSKIP` are Grokking rules nothing calls. This fixture certifies
    that the rule can be WRITTEN and DRIVEN, which is the step that was blocked.

    THE ROWS
      SK-1  the escaped spelling gives ONE term reading `//`
      SK-2  the two-literal spelling gives TWO terms reading `/` -- the contrast that picks SK-1
      SK-3  the fixture's own input really does carry a line comment. Anti-vacuity: without it,
            SK-4 would pass against an input with nothing in it to match
      SK-4  driving it reaches the foot -- no crash, no spin
      SK-5  ⚠ PINNED AT THE WALL. The terms read their DEFINITIONS after the drive. F-83.  */
Start();
register(Grokking);
define
    lcEsc     "/\/" commentBody="\n"};
    lcTwo     "/" "/" commentBody="\n"};
    ;
register(TestStuff);
define
    skIn=(// a real line comment
AFTER#);
    ;
search Grokking TestStuff list;

cerr "SK-3 the input":;
dumpContents(skIn);

cerr "SK-1 escaped spelling":;
dumpContents(lcEsc);
cerr "SK-2 two-literal spelling":;
dumpContents(lcTwo);

lcEsc(skIn);
cerr "SK-5 after the drive":;
dumpContents(lcEsc);

cerr "SKIPT SENTINEL":;
stop();
